# stressfs.quirks -- known-handler-behaviour database (M3,
# docs/implementation-plan.md section 3's M3 entry).
#
# Plain text, hand-parseable on a stock AmigaDOS machine. Same format
# and the same semantics as ~/src/devsoak/devsoak.quirks, per the
# implementation plan's section 0 decision table. One "quirk" block per
# entry:
#
#   quirk   <id>
#   match   key=value key<value key>=value ...   (repeatable; ALL
#                                                  conditions across ALL
#                                                  match lines in the
#                                                  entry must hold - AND)
#   because <free text, may wrap onto further indented lines>
#   action  <verb> <args...>                     (repeatable)
#   status  confirmed|reported|suspected
#
# '#' comments and blank lines are ignored. Match keys understood by
# stressfs's parser (amiga/lib/quirks.c): name=STR (case-insensitive
# exact match on the handler's short name as it appears in its own
# "$VER:" string -- "fs" for AmigaOS 3.2's FastFileSystem),
# version</<=/>/>=/= N and revision</<=/>/>=/= N (from that same
# "$VER:" string), id~STR (case-insensitive substring of the whole
# "$VER:" string), kick</<=/>/>=/= N (the running Kickstart's
# exec.library lib_Version), dos</<=/>/>=/= N (the running dos.library
# version), dostype=STR (case-insensitive exact match on the volume's
# on-disk DosType, e.g. "DOS1" or "PFS3" -- added at M5 so a
# ROM-resident handler with no "$VER:" string to scan, such as FFS V40
# baked into an A3000/A4000-profile Kickstart 3.1 ROM, can still be
# matched; see amiga/include/stressfs/quirks.h for the shorthand
# convention this uses for a DosType's non-printable low byte). Any
# other key is not evaluable by this parser: an entry
# using one is flagged unmatchable at load time, printed as a warning,
# and can then only ever be applied via FORCEQUIRK.
#
# Actions (exactly three, each with a defined final verdict -- see
# amiga/include/stressfs/quirks.h):
#   skip <test-id>                            the test is not executed
#                                             at all -> `unsupported`
#   downgrade <test-id>                       raw `fail` -> `deviation`,
#                                             whatever shape the failure
#                                             took
#   expect-deviation <test-id> <res1>/<res2>  raw `fail` -> `deviation`
#                                             only if the observed
#                                             dp_Res1/dp_Res2 matches the
#                                             declared pair ('*' = any);
#                                             any other failure stays a
#                                             raw `fail`
# A test-id target may end in '*' for a prefix match. The ids are the
# conform table's own test ids (amiga/src/conform.c) --
# ci/quirks-check.sh cross-references the two.
#
# Status policy: only "confirmed" entries are applied automatically.
# "reported" (seen in the wild or documented elsewhere, not reproduced
# by us) and "suspected" (a guess, or a first failure stressfs itself
# hit) entries are inert until named with FORCEQUIRK on the command
# line. Nothing in this file may be promoted to "confirmed" on the
# strength of a document alone -- only after it has actually been
# reproduced with stressfs against a real handler in a real emulator or
# on real hardware. Every "confirmed" entry below names the run that
# reproduced it. NOQUIRKS (-K) ignores this file entirely, confirmed
# entries included, so a covered deviation shows up as the raw finding.

# --- reproduced by stressfs against FFS 47.4 under Copperline ------------
# All four entries below were reproduced on 2026-10-22 with
# ci/copperline-m3.sh (headless, scripted `--run`/`--exit-on-return`;
# A1200 profile, Kickstart 47.x, an FFS "DOS\7" partition on an IDE
# hardfile), handler "$VER: fs 47.4 (16.6.2020)", dos.library 47. They
# are the raw `fail` lines M1/M2's own report against that handler
# produced; three of the four are on the proposal's "Documented quirks
# checked explicitly" list, which is what makes them worth seeding
# first.

quirk   ffs-is-filesystem-res2-garbage
match   name=fs version>=40
because ACTION_IS_FILESYSTEM answers correctly in dp_Res1 (DOSTRUE) but
        does not zero dp_Res2: FFS 47.4 returned dp_Res2 = 1027 where
        the manual's packet documentation says the second result is
        unused for this packet and callers that read IoErr() after a
        successful DoPkt() therefore see stale rubbish. This is the
        proposal's "ACTION_IS_FILESYSTEM dp_Res2-not-zeroed" quirk
        (docs/proposal.md, "Documented quirks checked explicitly"). The
        declared pair pins dp_Res1 to the CORRECT value (-1) and leaves
        dp_Res2 as '*' deliberately: the exact leftover value is
        whatever happened to be in the packet, so pinning it would make
        the entry stop applying for no good reason, while pinning
        dp_Res1 keeps the entry from ever covering a handler that got
        the actual answer wrong.
action  expect-deviation baseline-is-filesystem -1/*
status  confirmed

quirk   ffs-nil-action-not-known
match   name=fs version>=40
because ACTION_NIL (packet 0) is documented as a no-op a handler should
        reply to successfully; FFS 47.4 instead returns DOSFALSE with
        dp_Res2 = ERROR_ACTION_NOT_KNOWN (209), i.e. its packet switch
        has no case for it. Harmless in itself -- nothing in AmigaDOS
        sends ACTION_NIL -- but it is a real, reproducible protocol
        deviation and the baseline ladder sends it deliberately
        (docs/proposal.md, "Safe probing" point 1), so it must be
        declared rather than counted as a handler bug every run.
action  expect-deviation baseline-nil 0/209
status  confirmed

quirk   ffs-exclusive-root-lock-granted
match   name=fs version>=40
because The AmigaDOS Manual's packet chapter states an exclusive lock on
        the root directory is refused (docs/proposal.md lists
        "exclusive lock on the root refused" among the documented
        quirks to check explicitly). FFS 47.4 GRANTS it: the
        ACTION_LOCATE_OBJECT returns a real lock in dp_Res1 with
        dp_Res2 = 0. stressfs frees that lock again immediately -- see
        amiga/src/conform.c's run_locate_exclusive_root_refused(),
        which learned that the hard way -- so the rest of the run is
        unaffected. dp_Res1 is '*' because the value is a lock pointer
        and differs every run; dp_Res2 is pinned to 0 so that a genuine
        error return (which is what the manual describes) would NOT be
        covered by this entry.
action  expect-deviation locate-exclusive-root */0
status  confirmed

quirk   ffs-set-file-size-returns-size
match   name=fs version>=40
because ACTION_SET_FILE_SIZE is documented to return DOSTRUE/DOSFALSE in
        dp_Res1; FFS 47.4 returns the file's NEW SIZE there instead (20
        after growing stressfs's 15-byte fixture by 5, 5 after
        truncating it to 5), with dp_Res2 = 0. This is the proposal's
        "ACTION_SET_FILE_SIZE return-value" quirk. dp_Res1 is '*'
        because the value is a file size that depends on the fixture;
        dp_Res2 pinned to 0 keeps a real failure (nonzero dp_Res2) out
        of this entry's scope. Note the dos.library wrapper hides this:
        SetFileSize() reports success either way, which is exactly the
        proposal's reason for testing packets and wrappers as twins.
action  expect-deviation set-file-size-grow */0
action  expect-deviation set-file-size-truncate */0
status  confirmed

# --- reproduced by stressfs against FFS V40 under Copperline --------------
# M5 (docs/implementation-plan.md section 3's M5 entry). FFS V40 runs
# ROM-resident on an A3000/A4000-profile Kickstart 3.1 ROM (FastFileSystem
# is a resident module baked into that Kickstart, not an L:-loaded hunk
# file), so it has no classic hunk seglist for handler_id.c's "$VER:"
# scan to search -- `name=`/`version=` can never match it, which is
# exactly the gap the `pfs3-aio-identity-unexpressible` placeholder entry
# below flagged back at M3. M5 added the `dostype=` match key (see
# amiga/include/stressfs/quirks.h) to close it: DosType comes from the
# mount-time DosEnvec, available regardless of whether a "$VER:" string
# exists. All five entries below were reproduced on 2026-10-22 with
# ci/copperline-m5.sh (headless, scripted `--run`/`--exit-on-return`; A3000
# profile + `[scsi] controller = "a3000"` -- this ROM's scsi.device
# cannot drive Copperline's Gayle `[ide]` emulation, see
# docs/verdict-matrix.md's "Boot environments" section -- a fresh FFS
# "DOS1" partition built with amitools rdbtool+xdftool, no OS install
# needed since Copperline's `--run` stages its own boot volume),
# Kickstart 3.1 (40.68), dos.library 40, no "$VER:" string available
# (handler identified only by DosType + dos.library version). The first
# four are the SAME behaviours already confirmed against FFS 47.4 above,
# reproduced again on the older ROM-resident handler; the fifth
# (ACTION_END) is a genuinely new finding not present on FFS 47.4.

quirk   ffs-v40-is-filesystem-res2-garbage
match   dostype=DOS1 dos>=40
because Same finding as ffs-is-filesystem-res2-garbage above, reproduced
        directly against FFS V40 (ROM-resident, no "$VER:" string):
        ACTION_IS_FILESYSTEM answers dp_Res1 = DOSTRUE correctly but
        leaves dp_Res2 = 1027 un-zeroed.
action  expect-deviation baseline-is-filesystem -1/*
status  confirmed

quirk   ffs-v40-nil-action-not-known
match   dostype=DOS1 dos>=40
because Same finding as ffs-nil-action-not-known above, reproduced
        directly against FFS V40: ACTION_NIL returns DOSFALSE with
        dp_Res2 = ERROR_ACTION_NOT_KNOWN (209).
action  expect-deviation baseline-nil 0/209
status  confirmed

quirk   ffs-v40-exclusive-root-lock-granted
match   dostype=DOS1 dos>=40
because Same finding as ffs-exclusive-root-lock-granted above, reproduced
        directly against FFS V40: an exclusive ACTION_LOCATE_OBJECT on
        the root grants a real lock (dp_Res2 = 0) instead of being
        refused.
action  expect-deviation locate-exclusive-root */0
status  confirmed

quirk   ffs-v40-set-file-size-returns-size
match   dostype=DOS1 dos>=40
because Same finding as ffs-set-file-size-returns-size above, reproduced
        directly against FFS V40: ACTION_SET_FILE_SIZE returns the new
        file size in dp_Res1 (20 after growing the 15-byte fixture by
        5, 5 after truncating it back) rather than DOSTRUE/DOSFALSE.
action  expect-deviation set-file-size-grow */0
action  expect-deviation set-file-size-truncate */0
status  confirmed

quirk   ffs-v40-end-close-returns-32
match   dostype=DOS1 dos>=40
because NEW finding, not present against FFS 47.4: ACTION_END (the
        packet dos.library's Close() sends) is documented to return
        DOSTRUE/DOSFALSE in dp_Res1; FFS V40 instead returns 32 there,
        with dp_Res2 = 0, both for a file opened for output
        (ACTION_FINDOUTPUT) and one opened for input
        (ACTION_FINDINPUT). 32 is neither DOSTRUE (-1) nor a documented
        AmigaDOS error code, and it is not simply the fixture's byte
        count (the output fixture is 15 bytes, later grown to 20 and
        truncated to 5 by the SET_FILE_SIZE tests above; the input
        fixture, probe.txt, is a separate small file) -- it does not
        correlate with the ACTION_END packet number either (32 is not
        ACTION_END's own `dp_Type`). stressfs has not determined what
        32 actually encodes; it is recorded here as observed fact, not
        explained. dos.library's Close() (RKRM "Input and Output")
        reports success based on this same dp_Res1 the same way it
        treats every other packet's DOSTRUE, so a close() caller
        relying on the wrapper sees ordinary success either way --
        again the packet/wrapper-twin split the proposal calls for is
        what surfaces this at all. dp_Res1 is pinned to '*' (the value
        is a fixed constant here, 32, but pinning it narrowly risks the
        entry going stale if a different fixture size ever changes
        whatever 32 actually represents); dp_Res2 pinned to 0 keeps a
        real failure out of this entry's scope.
action  expect-deviation end-close-output */0
action  expect-deviation end-close-input */0
status  confirmed

# --- from the proposal's text only, NOT reproduced by stressfs ------------
# These entries are the ones the implementation plan asks to seed "from
# the proposal" -- no stressfs run has reproduced any of them, so by the
# status policy above none of them may be `confirmed`, and none of them
# auto-applies. Force one with FORCEQUIRK=<id> if a run needs it.

quirk   ffs-v40-flush-acks-early
match   name=fs version<=40
because docs/proposal.md, "Durability boundary for crash tests": "FFS <=
        V40 replied before the write finished, which the quirks file
        records". ACTION_FLUSH is supposed to be the acknowledgement
        point -- "data is acknowledged only when ACTION_FLUSH has
        returned" -- so a handler that replies to it before the data is
        on the medium breaks the durability boundary every layer-4
        crash test is judged against, and a power cut after a
        successful Flush() can lose acknowledged data on these
        versions. The action targets a durability test id
        (flush-durability-*) that the M1/M2 conform table does NOT yet
        contain: ACTION_FLUSH is not in phase 1's ~20-packet matrix,
        and proving this quirk needs the layer-4 fault machinery
        (Copperline's disk.fault `ignore` policy), which is phase 5.
        The entry is seeded now, inert, so that the durability test
        inherits a declared expectation the day it lands rather than
        being written against a handler whose behaviour nobody wrote
        down. Status is `reported`: the proposal states it as fact
        about real FFS versions, but this project has not reproduced
        it, and "nothing may be promoted to confirmed on the strength
        of a document alone".
action  downgrade flush-durability-*
status  reported

quirk   ffs-more-cache-count-in-res1
match   name=fs
because docs/proposal.md, "Documented quirks checked explicitly":
        "ACTION_MORE_CACHE returning the count in dp_Res1 as AddBuffers
        expects". The packet is documented as returning
        DOSTRUE/DOSFALSE, but AddBuffers reads the resulting buffer
        count out of dp_Res1 and real handlers oblige, so a strict
        reading of the manual would fail every handler in existence.
        ACTION_MORE_CACHE is not in phase 1's matrix (it is on the
        phase-2 list in the implementation plan's section 4), so this
        entry is inert and forward-looking, exactly like the flush
        entry above. Status `suspected` rather than `reported`: the
        proposal describes the convention, but which of dp_Res1's
        possible meanings a given FFS version actually implements is
        precisely what the phase-2 test will establish.
action  expect-deviation more-cache */0
status  suspected

quirk   pfs3-aio-identity-unexpressible
match   handler-dostype=PFS3
because Placeholder kept deliberately, and deliberately UNMATCHABLE, to
        exercise and document the parser's second failure mode (see
        amiga/include/stressfs/quirks.h): `handler-dostype` is a
        well-formed match key this build cannot evaluate, so this entry
        is flagged unmatchable at load, warned about once, and can only
        ever apply via FORCEQUIRK=pfs3-aio-identity-unexpressible. PFS3
        identity really is not expressible with the current key set:
        pfs3_handler's "$VER:" string is not reliably present in its
        segment list, so name=/version= cannot key on it, and the
        DosType that WOULD identify it ("PFS3") is not one of the keys
        the parser evaluates. M5 runs stressfs against PFS3-AIO and
        will either add a key or record the finding; until then this
        entry documents the gap in the file itself rather than in a
        comment nobody greps. No real action is attached beyond a skip
        of a test id that does not exist, so forcing it does nothing
        harmful.

        UPDATE at M5: the general gap this entry anticipated IS now
        closed -- `dostype=` (note: not `handler-dostype`, this entry's
        own made-up key name) is a real, evaluable match key as of M5
        (see the `dostype=DOS1` entries above, and
        amiga/include/stressfs/quirks.h). What did NOT happen is PFS3
        identification itself: PFS3-AIO never mounted long enough
        under Copperline this session to read ITS DosType back (see
        ci/m5/pfs3-aio-status.md for the full account), so there is
        still no reproduced PFS3 entry to add, and this placeholder is
        left exactly as M3 wrote it -- still deliberately using a key
        name (`handler-dostype`) the parser was never going to
        evaluate, still documenting the SPECIFIC gap of "PFS3 never
        got identified", which remains true even though the KIND of
        gap M3 guessed at (no evaluable key at all) no longer does.
action  skip pfs3-placeholder-never-matches
status  suspected
